module applied_sum_arm.neg_1

// A `match` ON AN APPLIED SUM -- refused BY NAME, and the name is the point.
//
// Until 2026-09-20 this said "this arm names a variant of a different sum than
// the value matched on". That sentence is about the PROGRAM and was a statement
// about the COMPILER: `Some` is registered under the generic `Option`, the
// scrutinee is an application, and the door compared type ids. The reader was
// sent looking for a sum mix-up that is not in their code.
//
// WHAT THE WAVE DID AND DELIBERATELY DID NOT DO. The door now finds the head
// through the DECLARATION, so the form is recognised -- and it is refused here,
// because the emitter cannot spell it: an applied sum reaching novac comes from
// the SHELL, with the oracle's tag names and value semantics, while novac emits
// its own spelling and a pointer access. Measured on this very file:
// `_novac_l2->tag` against a value struct, and an undeclared
// `NOVAC_TAG_..._Option_Some`.
//
// So recognition landed and compilation did not, ON PURPOSE. Letting the form
// through the checker with the emitter unable to carry it is the silent-
// acceptance class this compiler keeps closing -- and this same night it had
// already cost one wrong answer and two ices.
//
// A user's OWN generic sum never reaches this door: generic parameters are
// refused earlier (E2-b2). So this cell is about the shell's sums alone.

// NOVAC_EXPECT a `match` on an applied sum

fn from_option(o Option[int]) -> int => match o {
    Some(v) => v
    None => 0
}
